course: dictionary
lesson: 8825
title: Sherlock: a name, a person, a joke
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sherlock with a capital S is a name. Sherlock Holmes is a famous make-believe detective in stories by Arthur Conan Doyle. He solves crimes by noticing small details and thinking step by step, like following a trail of clues.
explain: Sherlock with a small s is a word people use in everyday talk. Someone who is good at finding things out can be called a Sherlock, so «Well done, Sherlock!» is praise.
explain: Sometimes people use it to tease. If you state something obvious, a friend might answer, «No kidding, Sherlock!» That is sarcasm: the words sound like they are impressed, but they really mean "that was obvious". Think about what was just said and how it was said.
fact: Sherlock Holmes lives at 221B Baker Street in London with his friend Dr Watson.
fact: Sherlock Holmes stories were written by Arthur Conan Doyle.
fact: «Sherlock» with a capital S is a name; with a small s it can mean a person who is good at finding things out.
fact: «No kidding, Sherlock!» is sarcastic: it teases someone for stating something obvious.
fact: In a logic puzzle, thinking "like Sherlock" means using clues one at a time to reach an answer.
exercise: In «Check your pocket, Sherlock.», what does Sherlock mean? | A famous castle in London | A loud noise | *A teasing name for someone acting like a detective | A kind of pocket
exercise: In the Sherlock Holmes stories, who is Sherlock's friend? | Mrs Doyle | *Dr Watson | Mr Pocket | Mr Baker
exercise: Ella says «No kidding, Sherlock!» after Leo states something obvious. What does she mean? | She has lost her pocket | She is hungry | *She is teasing him, because the point was obvious | She thinks he is a great detective
test: Where does Sherlock Holmes live in the stories? | Paris | *221B Baker Street, London | Edinburgh | A castle on a hill
test: Which phrase praises someone for finding things out? | "No kidding, Sherlock!" | *"Well done, Sherlock!" | "Check your pocket, Sherlock." | "Sherlock, you are obvious!"
exam: How does Sherlock Holmes solve crimes? | *By careful noticing and logical thinking | By shouting for help | Guessing at random | Sleeping on it
